  "background": "Ecclesiastes presents the words of Qohelet, often translated Teacher or Preacher, identified as “son of David, king in Jerusalem.” Jewish and Christian tradition commonly associated him with Solomon, especially because chapters 1–2 adopt a royal, Solomonic persona, while many scholars understand the final book as a later wisdom composition using that persona. The text itself should be allowed to carry the argument without making disputed authorship a test of faith.\n\nThe book repeatedly uses hebel, literally vapor or breath, for life that is brief, elusive, and difficult to master. “Under the sun” marks the Teacher’s sustained observation of mortal life. The final verses, 12:9–14, speak about the Teacher in the third person and provide a framing conclusion. Ecclesiastes belongs inside the larger biblical canon, so its limited observations about death and uncertainty should not be isolated from later resurrection hope.",
